name: std-fs-failure
description: "std.fs on a missing path and on the wrong type — the behaviour the four lanes AGREE on"
backends: [int, jvm, js, v2]
---

# `std.fs` failure behaviour — the agreed part

`specs/std-fs-os.md` §2.1 measures what every `std.fs` function does on a missing path and on a
path of the wrong type, across int / js / jvm / v2-native. Reported by **nadia** as
`std-fs-failure-contract` (2026-08-04): the spec mapped each name to a backend primitive and the
primitives disagree, so the contract a caller programmed against was "whatever the host does".

**This case gates only the rows where all four lanes agree**, and that boundary is the point. A
conformance case compares one expected output, so it cannot express a divergence — and the
divergences are real, filed, and deliberately NOT hidden here by weakening the case until it passes
everywhere:

- `listDir` on a missing path, and on a file, **raises on int / js / v2 and answers `List()` on
  jvm** — `jvm-listdir-answers-empty-where-every-other-lane-raises`. Gated by its sibling case
  `std-fs-failure-raises`, which declares the three lanes that agree.
- `v2 / native` erases the exception TYPE, so *missing* and *wrong type* cannot be told apart there
  even with a `catch`. Nothing gates that yet; it is in §2.1 and in the entry above.

What is below is therefore the FLOOR: behaviour a caller may rely on today on every lane.

## The total predicates answer, they do not raise

`exists` / `isFile` / `isDir` are the only total operations in `std.fs`, and this is what makes the
"guard before you read" convention possible at all.

**`exists` is TRUE FOR A DIRECTORY** — the row that matters most, and the second bug the reporter
found in their own code: guarding a `readFile` with `exists` alone lets a directory through, and it
then fails as `Is a directory`. Guard with `isFile` before `readFile`, `isDir` before `listDir`.

```scalascript
[exists, isFile, isDir, mkdirs, writeFile, deleteFile, mkdir](std/fs.ssc)

val root = "/tmp/ssc-fs-failure-case"
val dir  = root + "/adir"
val file = root + "/afile.txt"
val gone = root + "/does-not-exist"

mkdirs(dir)
writeFile(file, "x\n")

println("exists(missing)=" + exists(gone).toString)
println("isFile(missing)=" + isFile(gone).toString)
println("isDir(missing)=" + isDir(gone).toString)

println("exists(dir)=" + exists(dir).toString)
println("isFile(dir)=" + isFile(dir).toString)
println("isDir(dir)=" + isDir(dir).toString)

println("exists(file)=" + exists(file).toString)
println("isFile(file)=" + isFile(file).toString)
println("isDir(file)=" + isDir(file).toString)
```

## `deleteFile` and `mkdir` are already TOTAL, and the spec's mapping says otherwise

The JVM column of the table in §2 names `Files.createDirectory`, which raises when the directory
exists. No lane raises. `deleteFile` on a missing path is likewise a no-op everywhere. Both are
asserted here because a caller may rely on them and because the documented mapping predicts
something different — if a lane is ever "corrected" toward its primitive, this goes red.

```scalascript
deleteFile(gone)
println("deleteFile(missing)=ok")
mkdir(dir)
println("mkdir(existing)=ok")

deleteFile(file)
println("exists(file) after delete=" + exists(file).toString)
```
